<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lý Thường Kiệt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1019">1019</a>–1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lý Thường Kiệt was a general and court official of the Lý dynasty of Đại Việt (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>). In 1075–1076 he led a pre-emptive invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, sacking Yongzhou, and in 1077 he defeated the Song counter-invasion at the Như Nguyệt river north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>. The poem 'Nam quốc sơn hà', traditionally attributed to him, is often called Vietnam's first declaration of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ly-thuong-kiet.jpg" alt="Lý Thường Kiệt" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phương Huy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3ng_L%C3%BD_Th%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Dng_Ki%E1%BB%87t_t%E1%BA%A1i_B%E1%BA%A3o_t%C3%A0ng_l%E1%BB%8Bch_s%E1%BB%AD_qu%C3%A2n_s%E1%BB%B1_Vi%E1%BB%87t_Nam,_H%C3%A0_N%E1%BB%99i_n%C4%83m_2017_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lý Thường Kiệt was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, and took the surname Lý only when the emperor granted him the royal name. His original surname is disputed. A genealogy of the Ngô family and a text by Nhữ Bá Sĩ, written under the Nguyễn dynasty, make him Ngô Tuấn, son of the general Ngô An Ngữ and a descendant of Ngô Quyền; this view is the most widely accepted, though its sources are late.</p><p>Two steles of the Lý dynasty itself, of 1100 and 1159, instead name him Quách Tuấn, from An Xá village in Quảng Đức district, son of a commander-in-chief whom the Đại Việt sử lược calls Quách Thịnh Ích. Chinese histories call him Lý Thường Cát or Lý Thượng Cát, and he had a younger brother, Lý Thường Hiến. Nhữ Bá Sĩ tells that after his father died in 1031 the boy was raised by his aunt's husband, Tạ Đức, who taught him military books and married him to his niece Thuần Khanh. The scholar Hoàng Xuân Hãn warned that these details come from oral tradition and cannot be fully trusted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a>, Lý Thường Kiệt served as an official under three emperors, Lý Thái Tông, Lý Thánh Tông and Lý Nhân Tông. He took part in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> in 1069, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>–Lý War he raided the three Song provinces of Yongzhou, Qinzhou and Lianzhou in 1075 and 1076 and then defeated the Song invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> led by Gou Kui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-xie">Zhao Xie</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He may have written the poem Nam Quốc Sơn Hà, though its authorship is disputed. It was composed to rouse troops against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and according to the 20th-century historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-trong-kim">Trần Trọng Kim</a> he feared his soldiers were losing heart and presented the poem as the work of the gods. He was also the author of the Phạt Tống lộ bố văn, An Account of the Campaign to Punish the Song, another text directed against the Song dynasty. The poem remains well known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, where he is considered a national hero and is still worshipped as a deity at his shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lý Thường Kiệt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The general <b>Ly Thuong Kiet</b> raided Song China in 1075 and defeated a Song counter-invasion at the Nhu Nguyet river in 1077.</blockquote><a href="/state/ly-dynasty">Ly dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
